Startup trace: seven-character lines, and an in-place mode for a frozen screen

startup_debug=0x1 prints one line per startup entry. Two problems on raphael:

  * the full line is 73 characters and wraps onto two console lines, and the
    tail of a wedged boot lands on the very last line, which the display's
    rounded corner physically covers;
  * the panel console is a ring ("lines wrap back to the top instead of
    scrolling"), so once the log is longer than the screen the newest line is
    no longer the bottom one.

Reading the wedge point back from photographs therefore cost a day of guessing.

0x1 now prints "K<subsystem><func low16>" (seven characters, never wraps) and
0x2 prints "\rM<subsystem><func low16>", which overwrites a single console line
in place: the screen always shows exactly the last entry executed, whatever the
ring is doing. The low 16 bits resolve against the kernel's symbols.

--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -402,8 +402,15 @@ kernel_startup_initialize_upto(startup_subsystem_id_t upto)
 	while (cur < startup_entries_end && cur->subsystem <= upto) {
 		if ((startup_debug & STARTUP_DEBUG_VERBOSE) &&
 		    startup_phase >= STARTUP_SUB_KPRINTF) {
-			kprintf("%s[%d, rank %d]: %p(%p)\n", __func__,
-			    cur->subsystem, cur->rank, cur->func, cur->arg);
+			if (startup_debug & 0x2) {
+				/* SWEETTRACE in-place: the marker overwrites one
+				 * console line, so it survives ring wrapping. */
+				kprintf("\rM%02x%04x", (unsigned)cur->subsystem,
+				    (unsigned)((uintptr_t)cur->func & 0xffffu));
+			} else {
+				kprintf("K%02x%04x\n", (unsigned)cur->subsystem,
+				    (unsigned)((uintptr_t)cur->func & 0xffffu));
+			}
 		}
 		startup_phase = cur->subsystem - 1;
 		kernel_startup_log(cur->subsystem);
